Field notes, Week 3. The 'standup' ritual — ostensibly a 15-minute coordination meeting. But I've timed 12 sessions: average 38 minutes. The VP of Engineering speaks first, 60% of airtime. Updates become performances of alignment. Junior devs mirror his vocabulary: 'blockers,' 'velocity,' 'surface area.'
    --- message[1] role='researcher_b' ---
Classic 'ritualization of subordination' — Moore 1978. The standup isn't for information flow; it's a loyalty display. The vocabulary mirroring is what Bourdieu calls 'habitus acquisition' — junior members internalize the dominant group's categories of perception. Did you catch the 'parking lot' maneuver?
    --- message[2] role='researcher_a' ---
Yes — when a junior dev raised a structural concern ('Our CI pipeline fails 40% of the time'), the VP said 'Good catch, let's park that,' then never returned to it. Three sprints now. The 'parking lot' functions as a ritualized dismissal that preserves the appearance of openness.
    --- message[3] role='researcher_b' ---
And the 'retro' meetings? Separate ritual?
    --- message[4] role='researcher_a' ---
Separate but complementary. Retro is the 'safety valve' — controlled venting. Action items are generated but ownership diffuses. 'Let's create a ticket' becomes the universal deferral. The same structural issues recur quarter after quarter. The rituals produce the *appearance* of continuous improvement while stabilizing the power structure.
    --- message[5] role='researcher_b' ---
So the influence mechanism isn't overt coercion — it's the temporal architecture. Meetings consume agency time, rituals legitimize hierarchy, and the vocabulary itself constrains what problems can be articulated. The 'CI pipeline' issue can't be framed as 'management underinvestment' because that category doesn't exist in the sanctioned lexicon.
    --- message[6] role='researcher_a' ---
Exactly. The ethnographer's job: make the invisible grammar visible. Not to 'fix' the culture — that's not our role — but to describe the mechanism so insiders can recognize it if they choose.
